    {
      "step_id": "tile_python_conditionals_for_loops.s05",
      "type": "explainer",
      "concept_ids": [
        "python.for_loops"
      ],
      "eyebrow": "Iteration",
      "title": "Visiting every item: the for loop",
      "lede": "Process every delivery time in a list without writing the same code over and over.",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "block_id": "tile_python_conditionals_for_loops.s05.h1",
          "text": "Visiting every item: the for loop",
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "block_id": "tile_python_conditionals_for_loops.s05.p1",
          "text": "Once delivery times are collected in a list, you'll want to do something with every single one of them, not just one at a time by hand. Python's for statement iterates over the items of any sequence — such as a list or a string — in the order they appear. Each pass binds the loop variable to the next item:\n\nfor minutes in delivery_times:\n    print(minutes)\n\nThis prints every delivery time in delivery_times, one per pass.",
          "role": "mechanism",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "python.for_loops"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "python.for_loops#f1",
              "python.for_loops#f2"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "python.for_loops#tb1",
              "python.for_loops#tb2"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "prose",
          "block_id": "tile_python_conditionals_for_loops.s05.p2",
          "text": "The header line ends with a colon, and the statements to repeat are indented beneath it. You can combine a for loop with a conditional check inside the body:\n\nfor minutes in delivery_times:\n    if minutes > 45:\n        print(minutes)\n\nWith delivery_times = [32, 45, 21, 60, 18], this prints only 60 — the one delivery time above 45 minutes.",
          "role": "syntax",
          "lineage": {
            "concept_ids": [
              "python.for_loops"
            ],
            "fact_ids": [
              "python.for_loops#f2",
              "python.for_loops#f3"
            ],
            "teaching_block_ids": [
              "python.for_loops#tb3",
              "python.for_loops#tb4"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "process_flow",
          "block_id": "tile_python_conditionals_for_loops.s05.pf1",
          "title": "How a for loop steps through delivery_times",
          "direction": "TB",
